[Prov. Eng.]

'''whitewash''' (hwīt′wosh), ''n.'' '''1.''' A wash or liquid composition for whitening something. {{smaller|Especially—(''a'') A wash for making the skin fair.}}
{{smaller block|The clergy . . . were very much taken up in reforming the female world; I have heard a whole sermon against a ''whitewash.'' {{right|''Addison'', Guardian, No. 116.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') A composition of quicklime and water, or, for more careful work, of whiting, size, and water, used for whitening the plaster of walls, woodwork, etc., or as a freshening coating for any surface. It is not used for fine work.}}
{{smaller block|Some dilapidations there are to be made good; . . . but a little glazing, painting, ''whitewash'', and plaster will make it [a house] last thy time. {{right|''Vanbrugh'', Relapse, v. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' False coloring, as of character, alleged services, etc.; the covering up of wrong-doing or defects: as, the investigating committee applied a thick coat of ''whitewash.'' [Colloq.]—'''3.''' In ''base-ball'' and other games, a contest in which one side fails to score. [Colloq.]

'''whitewash''' (hwīt′wosh), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''whitewashed'', ppr. ''whitewashing.'' [< ''whitewash'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To cover with a white liquid composition, as with lime and water, etc.
{{smaller block|There were workmen pulling down some of the old hangings and replacing them with others, altering, repairing, scrubbing, painting, and ''white-washing.'' {{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xliii.}}}}
'''2.''' To make white; give a fair external appearance to; attempt to clear from imputations; attempt to restore the reputation of. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|A ''white-washed'' Jacobite; that is, one who, having been long a non-juror, . . . had lately qualified himself to act as a justice, by taking the oaths to Government. {{right|''Scott'', Rob Roy, vii.}}}}
